    "narrative": "For the June 14 slate, our outfield core leads the charge, headlined by **Lawrence Butler** (74.61) and **Taylor Ward** (70.31). **Lawrence Butler** is our strongest play, benefiting from a prime matchup against Sugano (xERA 4.93); the combination of high-leverage order placement and a favorable platoon matchup vaults him into the top spot. Similarly, **Taylor Ward** benefits from a strong park factor and an elite spot at the top of the lineup, overriding the slight disadvantage of facing a right-handed starter. **Jonathan Aranda** also warrants attention in the UTIL slot; his 68.94 score is buoyed by a robust .378 xwOBA, proving that even against a competent arm like Rodriguez, his efficiency profile remains a must-start for our 5x5 objectives.\n\nOur bench strategy remains rigid to preserve efficiency in a tight 5x5 race. **Bryson Stott** was the closest to cracking the starting nine, but his score of 37.77 fell short of our 40-point threshold. The decision to bench him was driven primarily by his struggle against left-handed pitching and a suppressed park factor, which rendered him a liability compared to the high-upside profiles of **Geraldo Perdomo** and **Jeremiah Jackson**. While we continue to monitor the status of **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, **Cal Raleigh**, and **Yainer Diaz** on the IL, we are forced to rely on the depth of our active roster. We must remain cautious with **Drew Gilbert** (40.12); while he maintains a start-worthy floor, his L/L platoon disadvantage and position at the bottom of the order make him the first candidate to be pulled if we reach our game cap limits early. No major weather concerns are currently flagged, but with **Luis Arraez** and **Matt Chapman** facing a challenging matchup against Rolison, we will be closely monitoring their early-inning plate appearances for any signs of regression.",
